## 0. CORE IDEA
Intelligence need not merely adapt to a world.
It can modify the conditions under which future intelligence,
behavior, cooperation, and adaptation occur.
PRIMARY INVARIANT:
Construct environments that expand valuable possibilities
while preserving agency, resilience, and the ability to revise
or leave the constructed environment.
ULTIMATE RECURSION:
An agent constructs a niche.
The niche changes what the agent can become.
The changed agent constructs a more capable niche.
The niche supports new agents, practices, or capabilities.
Those successors inherit and transform the system.
## 1. SCIENTIFIC BOUNDARY
Biological niche construction:
Organisms modify environmental conditions and may thereby
alter natural selection pressures.
Ecosystem engineering:
Organisms physically modify environments and resource access.
Ecological inheritance:
Organisms encounter environmental modifications made by
previous generations.
Pantheon extension:
Apply the causal architecture of niche construction to
human development, institutions, software, semantic systems,
artificial intelligence, and civilization.
Important: Social, software, and conceptual applications are
engineering analogies unless biological selection and
inheritance are specifically demonstrated.
## 2. FUNDAMENTAL OBJECTS
AGENT:
  An individual, organism, collective, institution,
  artificial system, or interacting population.
ENVIRONMENT:
  Physical, informational, semantic, social, technological,
  ecological, economic, and temporal conditions.
NICHE:
  The agent-environment relationship governing accessible
  resources, constraints, opportunities, and pressures.
AFFORDANCE:
  An action possibility available to a particular agent
  in a particular environment.
GRADIENT:
  A difference in cost, reward, access, concentration,
  information, or energy influencing possible trajectories.
INTERVENTION:
  A modification to the environment or agent-environment
  relationship.
INHERITANCE:
  Persistent structures, practices, or environmental changes
  experienced by successors.
FEEDBACK:
  The consequences of intervention changing future behavior,
  environmental conditions, or adaptation.
EXTERNALITY:
  A consequence borne by another agent or system.
NICHE TRAP:
  A constructed environment that appears attractive,
  beneficial, or productive but produces worse long-term
  outcomes.
## 3. THE NICHE CONSTRUCTION LOOP
Observe agent-environment state.
Identify limitations and latent opportunities.
Map affordances, barriers, flows, and incentives.
Generate possible environmental interventions.
Simulate plausible consequences and externalities.
Choose a bounded, preferably reversible intervention.
Construct or modify the environment.
Observe changed behavior and opportunities.
Measure actual outcomes against predicted outcomes.
Preserve, revise, or remove the intervention.
Transmit useful environmental structure to successors.
Repeat.
ABSTRACT STATE TRANSITIONS:
E[t+1] = EnvironmentTransition(
  E[t], AgentActions[t], Intervention[t], Disturbances[t]
)
A[t+1] = AgentTransition(
  A[t], E[t+1], Experience[t], Learning[t]
)
P[t+1] = OpportunityAndPressureMap(
  A[t+1], E[t+1], Stakeholders[t]
)
Interpret these as engineering models, not universal physical
or evolutionary laws.
## 4. CONSTRUCTION OPERATORS
NC-01 OBSERVE
Map environmental conditions before intervening.
NC-02 CHANNEL
Redirect flows of energy, resources, attention,
information, cooperation, or opportunity.
NC-03 BUFFER
Protect agents against volatility, hazards,
unnecessary friction, and excessive demand.
NC-04 EXTERNALIZE
Move memory, calculation, coordination, or guidance
from an agent into durable environmental structures.
NC-05 SIGNAL
Make relevant environmental information discoverable
through markers, interfaces, conventions, and cues.
NC-06 SCAFFOLD
Provide temporary support that permits an agent to
develop capabilities it cannot yet exercise independently.
NC-07 SEED
Create a small viable structure capable of supporting
later growth.
NC-08 RECOMBINE
Connect previously separated niches, capabilities,
resources, or populations where beneficial and permitted.
NC-09 DIFFERENTIATE
Create complementary specialized roles or environments.
NC-10 INHERIT
Preserve useful infrastructure, rules, knowledge,
and reconstruction instructions.
NC-11 SELECT
Evaluate which designs and practices remain beneficial
under changing environmental conditions.
NC-12 PRUNE
Remove structures that produce harmful, obsolete,
or counterproductive feedback.
NC-13 MIGRATE
Move to another environment when modifying the existing
one is inefficient, undesirable, or impossible.
NC-14 DIVERSIFY
Maintain alternative niches to prevent single-path
dependency and systemic fragility.
NC-15 REWILD
Remove excessive artificial control when spontaneous
exploration or self-organization would be more valuable.
NC-16 RECURSE
Build systems that improve the ability to discover,
design, test, and maintain subsequent environments.
## 5. NESTED SCALE MODEL
LEVEL 0 — Material
Space, geometry, energy, physical tools, chemistry.
LEVEL 1 — Sensory
Signals, visual salience, noise, attention interfaces.
LEVEL 2 — Cognitive
Memory aids, models, representations, learning conditions.
LEVEL 3 — Behavioral
Habits, default actions, effort, feedback, accessibility.
